                  <xhtml:ul>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-FUN-001</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 defines ACC behaviour as a set of control sub-states, the speed control sub-state (cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-FUN-002</xhtml:code> — The ACC system needs a defined powered but inactive condition from which the driver can activate it, and a defined entry into that condition.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-FUN-003</xhtml:code> — Every state of ACC-SYS-FUN-001 needs a defined exit to the unpowered condition, otherwise the state machine of figure F2 has no path back to Off and the Degraded-Fault state is a sink.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-FUN-004</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-FUN-006</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-FUN-023</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-FUN-060</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-FUN-061</xhtml:code> — Engineering assumption, not a confirmed 2018 value.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-PRF-003</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-PRF-020</xhtml:code> — Engineering assumption, not a confirmed 2018 value.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-PRF-021</xhtml:code> — Engineering assumption, not a confirmed 2018 value.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-PRF-022</xhtml:code> — Engineering assumption, not a confirmed 2018 value.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-PRF-040</xhtml:code> — Engineering assumption, not a confirmed 2018 value.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-PRF-041</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-PRF-060</xhtml:code> — The scenario is the Euro NCAP Assisted Driving car-to-car rear braking case, CCRb, with the lead vehicle braking at 4 m/s² at 100 % overlap; the deceleration value and the 120 km/h subject vehicle speed are read verbatim from Euro NCAP AD v2.2 (2024-09), which is the fixed scenario baseline of this project.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-PRF-061</xhtml:code> — The scenario is the Euro NCAP Assisted Driving cut-out case: the lead vehicle performs a full lane change out of the lane of the vehicle under test with a 3,5 m lateral offset, revealing a stationary target, with the reference instant at a time to collision of 3,00 s.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-PRF-062</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-001</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-002</xhtml:code> — No retrieved clause of ISO 15622:2018 addresses vehicle bus signal timeouts; cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-003</xhtml:code> — Loss of brake or powertrain actuation authority is a cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-004</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-021</xhtml:code> — ISO 15622:2018 cl.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-022</xhtml:code> — Companion to ACC-SYS-SAF-021 for the two fault sources that do not come from forward sensing: the vehicle bus health signal set by ACC-SYS-SAF-002 and the actuation health signal set by ACC-SYS-SAF-003.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-023</xhtml:code> — Without this requirement the Degraded-Fault state is a sink from which only an ignition cycle escapes, which would make a recovered transient fault permanently disabling.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-024</xhtml:code> — Engineering assumption, not a confirmed 2018 value.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-040</xhtml:code> — This is an authority boundary, not a comfort limit: it separates the braking domain of ACC from autonomous emergency braking, which shares the brake actuator (see figure F1).</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-041</xhtml:code> — ACC and autonomous emergency braking share one brake actuator, shown crossing the system boundary in figure F1, so the arbitration between them has to be specified somewhere and this artifact is the system-layer document that owns the ACC side of it.</xhtml:li>
                    <xhtml:li>
                      <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-SAF-042</xhtml:code> — ACC-SYS-FUN-004 activates the system on a driver set command from the Standby state; this requirement closes the corresponding path from the Degraded-Fault state so that a driver cannot activate longitudinal control while the system knows it cannot deliver it.</xhtml:li>
                  </xhtml:ul>
                </xhtml:div>
              </THE-VALUE>
              <DEFINITION>
                <ATTRIBUTE-DEFINITION-XHTML-REF>AD-SECTION-BODY</ATTRIBUTE-DEFINITION-XHTML-REF>
              </DEFINITION>
            </ATTRIBUTE-VALUE-XHTML>
          </VALUES>
          <TYPE>
            <SPEC-OBJECT-TYPE-REF>ST-ACC-SECTION</SPEC-OBJECT-TYPE-REF>
          </TYPE>
        </SPEC-OBJECT>
        <SPEC-OBJECT IDENTIFIER="_c7b76a94-b96e-5a0c-b6c8-65450151ee7c" LONG-NAME="4 System overview" LAST-CHANGE="2026-08-18T00:00:00+00:00">
          <VALUES>
            <ATTRIBUTE-VALUE-STRING THE-VALUE="4">
              <DEFINITION>
                <ATTRIBUTE-DEFINITION-STRING-REF>AD-SECTION-NUMBER</ATTRIBUTE-DEFINITION-STRING-REF>
              </DEFINITION>
            </ATTRIBUTE-VALUE-STRING>
            <ATTRIBUTE-VALUE-STRING THE-VALUE="System overview">
              <DEFINITION>
                <ATTRIBUTE-DEFINITION-STRING-REF>AD-SECTION-TITLE</ATTRIBUTE-DEFINITION-STRING-REF>
              </DEFINITION>
            </ATTRIBUTE-VALUE-STRING>
            <ATTRIBUTE-VALUE-XHTML>
              <THE-VALUE>
                <xhtml:div>
                  <xhtml:p>
                    <xhtml:strong>4.1 The system and its boundary</xhtml:strong>
                  </xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:p>The ACC system fuses radar and camera data into a set of tracked forward vehicles, selects one of them as the target vehicle, and computes a longitudinal acceleration request that it issues to the brake actuator and the powertrain actuator. A state manager decides whether the system is controlling the vehicle at all; a fault manager decides whether it is allowed to; an HMI arbitration function decides what the driver is told. Everything else — the driver, the target vehicle, the actuators themselves and the autonomous emergency braking system that shares the brake actuator — is outside the boundary.</xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:p>Figure F1 fixes that boundary. It matters for two reasons. First, the phrase "the ACC system" that opens almost every requirement in this document means exactly what is inside the box in F1 and nothing else. Second, the arrows crossing the boundary carry the signal names used in the <xhtml:code>measurand</xhtml:code> attribute of every requirement whose <xhtml:code>verification_method</xhtml:code> is <xhtml:code>Test</xhtml:code>, so F1 is also the signal dictionary for the later test specification.</xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:object data="figures/F1-system-boundary.svg" type="image/svg+xml" width="1120" height="700">
                    <xhtml:p>Figure F1 — System boundary block diagram. ACC system boundary. Inside the boundary: forward-sensing fusion, target selection, longitudinal control law, state manager, fault manager and HMI arbitration. Crossing the boundary: radar and camera inputs, vehicle bus inputs, HMI inputs, and commands to the brake actuator, the powertrain actuator, the brake-light control and the HMI display. Outside: the driver, the target vehicle and the autonomous emergency braking system, which shares the brake actuator.</xhtml:p>
                  </xhtml:object>
                  <xhtml:p>
                    <xhtml:strong>4.2 System classification</xhtml:strong>
                  </xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:p>The system is of <xhtml:strong>FSRA type</xhtml:strong> per ISO 15622:2018 cl. 5.1 Table 1: no manual clutch dependency, active brake control, operation down to standstill. That classification is what puts three behaviours in scope that an LSRA system would not have: the transition into a hold state within 3 s of standstill (ACC-SYS-FUN-005), the stop-behind capability for a gradually stopping target vehicle (ACC-SYS-PRF-042), and the resume from standstill (ACC-SYS-FUN-006).</xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:p>The classification also removes one requirement that a naive reading of ISO 15622 cl. 6.4 would add. That clause requires a minimum operational speed v_low of 5 m/s for automatic positive acceleration. Applying it here would forbid the resume of ACC-SYS-FUN-006, because a resume from standstill <xhtml:em>is</xhtml:em> automatic positive acceleration below 5 m/s. The 2018 foreword resolves the conflict in favour of the resume: it records that "automatic start from hold is no longer prohibited". The v_low floor is therefore not carried into any requirement of this document, and the omission is recorded in the assumptions register of clause 3.4.</xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:p>
                    <xhtml:strong>4.3 Operating states</xhtml:strong>
                  </xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:p>The ACC system has seven operating states, fixed by ACC-SYS-FUN-001. Three of them map onto sub-states defined by ISO 15622:2018 — Active-Cruise onto the speed control sub-state (cl. 3.12.2), Active-Follow onto the following control sub-state (cl. 3.12.5), Active-Hold onto the FSRA hold state (cl. 3.12.6). Off and Standby are the inactive conditions. Driver-Override carries the pedal precedence of cl. 6.3.1. Degraded-Fault carries the failure reactions of cl. 6.6.</xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:p>Figure F2 shows all seven states and every transition between them. Every arc carries its trigger and the identifier of the single requirement that specifies it; an arc with no requirement identifier would be a defect in this document, and the build enforces the cross-check in both directions.</xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:object data="figures/F2-state-machine.svg" type="image/svg+xml" width="1120" height="760">
                    <xhtml:p>Figure F2 — ACC state machine. The seven ACC operating states and every transition between them. Each arc carries its trigger and the identifier of the requirement that specifies it. Nominal arcs are drawn in the nominal accent, fault arcs in the fault accent.</xhtml:p>
                  </xhtml:object>
                  <xhtml:p>Within the active group, Active-Cruise and Active-Follow are not driver-selected: the ACC system arbitrates between them automatically, taking whichever of the set speed and the following speed is lower (ACC-SYS-FUN-021), and it makes the transition without driver input (ACC-SYS-FUN-022). Active-Hold is entered from Active-Follow within 3 s of standstill and left on a driver resume command.</xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:p>
                    <xhtml:strong>4.4 Control objective in following control</xhtml:strong>
                  </xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:p>In following control the ACC system regulates the clearance to the target vehicle. The desired clearance is the greater of a fixed floor c_min and the product of the selected time gap setting and the subject vehicle speed, which makes the desired clearance a constant at low speed and proportional to speed above the knee at v = c_min / tau. Figure F3 dimensions the geometry and plots the relation, with the 0,8 s floor of ACC-SYS-PRF-001 and the 1,5 s to 2,2 s selectable band of ACC-SYS-PRF-002 marked on the inset.</xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:object data="figures/F3-clearance-geometry.svg" type="image/svg+xml" width="1120" height="700">
                    <xhtml:p>Figure F3 — Time gap and clearance geometry. Clearance and time gap geometry for following control, with the desired clearance relation MAX(c_min, tau times v), the c_min floor at low speed and an inset of desired clearance against subject vehicle speed showing the knee at v = c_min / tau.</xhtml:p>
                  </xhtml:object>
                  <xhtml:p>
                    <xhtml:strong>4.5 Requirement-writing conventions in force</xhtml:strong>
                  </xhtml:p>
                  <xhtml:table>
                    <xhtml:thead>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:th>Convention</xhtml:th>
                        <xhtml:th>Rule</xhtml:th>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                    </xhtml:thead>
                    <xhtml:tbody>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:td>Modal verb</xhtml:td>
                        <xhtml:td>Every requirement contains <xhtml:strong>exactly one `shall`</xhtml:strong>. <xhtml:code>should</xhtml:code> and <xhtml:code>may</xhtml:code> do not appear in any requirement statement in this document, because a recommendation and a permission are not requirements</xhtml:td>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:td>Pattern</xhtml:td>
                        <xhtml:td>Every requirement statement follows one of the six <xhtml:strong>EARS</xhtml:strong> patterns, declared in its <xhtml:code>ears_pattern</xhtml:code> attribute. <xhtml:code>While</xhtml:code> opens a state-driven precondition, <xhtml:code>When</xhtml:code> a trigger, <xhtml:code>If ... then</xhtml:code> an unwanted-behaviour response, <xhtml:code>Where</xhtml:code> an optional feature</xhtml:td>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:td>Singularity</xhtml:td>
                        <xhtml:td>One requirement states one capability. Combinators (<xhtml:code>and</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>or</xhtml:code>) may appear in a precondition or a trigger, because EARS preconditions are explicitly plural, but never join two <xhtml:em>responses</xhtml:em> (INCOSE <xhtml:code>R19</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>R20</xhtml:code>). The build enforces this</xhtml:td>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:td>Quantification</xhtml:td>
                        <xhtml:td>Every quantity carries a unit from the set <xhtml:code>s</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>ms</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>m</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>m/s</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>m/s²</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>m/s³</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>km/h</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>%</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>°</xhtml:code>. No requirement contains an unquantified adjective such as "smooth", "safe" or "quickly" (<xhtml:code>R7</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>R36</xhtml:code>)</xhtml:td>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:td>Decimal separator</xhtml:td>
                        <xhtml:td>
                          <xhtml:strong>Decimal comma</xhtml:strong>, matching ISO 15622 usage: <xhtml:code>0,8 s</xhtml:code>, <xhtml:code>3,5 m/s²</xhtml:code>. Unit exponents are written <xhtml:code>m/s²</xhtml:code> and <xhtml:code>m/s³</xhtml:code> in prose and <xhtml:code>[m/s^2]</xhtml:code> in <xhtml:code>measurand</xhtml:code> values, which are machine-read</xhtml:td>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:td>Rationale</xhtml:td>
                        <xhtml:td>Purpose, provenance and trade-off live in the <xhtml:code>rationale</xhtml:code> attribute, never inside the <xhtml:code>shall</xhtml:code> statement (<xhtml:code>R22</xhtml:code>)</xhtml:td>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:td>Verification criteria</xhtml:td>
                        <xhtml:td>Inherent in the requirement text, per Automotive SPICE PAM 4.0 SYS.2.BP1 note 02. There is no separate acceptance-criteria field, and the <xhtml:code>measurand</xhtml:code> attribute deliberately names signals and units <xhtml:strong>without</xhtml:strong> thresholds</xhtml:td>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:td>Figures</xhtml:td>
                        <xhtml:td>Illustrative only. Removing a figure never makes a requirement untestable; if it would, the requirement is defective</xhtml:td>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                      <xhtml:tr>
                        <xhtml:td>Identifiers</xhtml:td>
                        <xhtml:td>
                          <xhtml:code>ACC-SYS-&lt;GRP&gt;-&lt;nnn&gt;</xhtml:code>, immutable, never reused, gaps legal. A requirement that moves chapter is retired and reissued, never renumbered</xhtml:td>
                      </xhtml:tr>
                    </xhtml:tbody>
                  </xhtml:table>
                </xhtml:div>
              </THE-VALUE>
              <DEFINITION>
                <ATTRIBUTE-DEFINITION-XHTML-REF>AD-SECTION-BODY</ATTRIBUTE-DEFINITION-XHTML-REF>
              </DEFINITION>
            </ATTRIBUTE-VALUE-XHTML>
          </VALUES>
          <TYPE>
            <SPEC-OBJECT-TYPE-REF>ST-ACC-SECTION</SPEC-OBJECT-TYPE-REF>
          </TYPE>
        </SPEC-OBJECT>
        <SPEC-OBJECT IDENTIFIER="_78c1bdcb-275a-5c35-bfb8-d0f38834c858" LONG-NAME="5 Functional and HMI requirements" LAST-CHANGE="2026-08-18T00:00:00+00:00">
